, Stockholm"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/73612"} "93 from 15 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The format of the event was four rounds of pools.  Bouts were for one touch, and a double touch counted as a defeat for both fencers.  If there was a tie in the number of victories and if the number of fencers who were tied was greater than the number who could qualify, the tie was resolved by using the results of the tied fencers in their bouts with each other, as if another pool had been fenced. The same method was used for resolving ties in the final pool.  For example, in the finals, " [:a {:href "/athletes/21277"} "Philippe Le Hardy de Beaulieu"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/21227"} "Victor Boin"] " were tied with four victories for third place in the pool. Le Hardy de Beaulieu had beaten Boin in the pool, and so he was the one who won the bronze medal."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The épée events were somewhat weak competitions, as the best épée fencers in the world were the French and the Italians. The French boycotted the entire Olympic fencing competition, and the Italians refused to compete in the épée, upset with the rules. The Italian Fencing Federation had proposed that the blade of the épée be lengthened to 94 cm and, when this proposal was rejected, they refused to participate in the épée events."] "\n\n" [:p {} "With the French and Italians absent, the Belgians did well, advancing four fencers to the final.  " [:a {:href "/athletes/21218"} "Paul Anspach"] " of Belgium won the gold medal.  Anspach competed as a fencer at the Olympics of 1908, 1912, 1920, and 1924, winning five Olympic medals as follows: " [:a {:href "/results/73233"} "1908 épée team"] " bronze; 1912 épée individual gold; " [:a {:href "/results/73777"} "1912 épée team"] " gold; " [:a {:href "/results/74037"} "1920 épée team"] " silver; and " [:a {:href "/results/75902"} "1924 épée team"] " silver."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thea